WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:07.280
The Python course in the IT Specialist series&nbsp;
introduces one to Python, a very versatile and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:12.680
popular programming language, and maps to&nbsp;
the IT Specialist-Python exam. The course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:17.280
will introduce you to the basics of Python and&nbsp;
some of its uses and will go through the exam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:24.280
objectives from start to finish. To help put a&nbsp;
little real-world application into this course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:29.080
many of the code examples you will see will be&nbsp;
snippets of game development, one of the many uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:35.600
for Python. You will learn about other uses in the&nbsp;
next topic, What is Python? This course consists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:41.560
of the videos you are watching, practice labs,&nbsp;
pre- and post-assessment tests for each domain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:47.280
and a project workbook with exercises geared&nbsp;
toward helping you solidify what you will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:53.560
learn in these videos. There are six domains&nbsp;
in this IT Specialist Python exam. They are:

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:57.080
Operations using Data Types and Operators

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:00.000
Flow Control with Decisions and Loops

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.280
Input and Output Operations

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.600
Code Documentation and Structure

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.200
Troubleshooting and Error Handling

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.840
Operations using Modules and Tools

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:18.640
For the exam, ensure you obtain a copy of the&nbsp;
objectives, as shown here, and I would download&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:26.280
a copy as I have done here as the website version&nbsp;
is hard to read. Pay close attention to the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:35.400
words you see in each second group of objectives,&nbsp;
as those words play into the types of questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:43.040
you can expect on the exam. For example, when you&nbsp;
see construct and analyze, be ready to determine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:49.240
the code needed to reach a specified outcome&nbsp;
and be able to take an existing piece of code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:54.920
and answer questions about what the code does.&nbsp;
Don’t worry. We will show you some examples of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.920 --> 00:02:02.160
analyzing code as we cover these topics. Also,&nbsp;
if you have not yet done so, I would suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.640
taking the 90-minute Introduction to Programming&nbsp;
course or having an equivalent level of knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:13.000
before tackling this course. The Introduction&nbsp;
to Programming course covers basic concepts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:20.000
such as properties, methods, variables, and loops,&nbsp;
without emphasizing any language. If you already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:28.640
have that knowledge, great. If not, we will still&nbsp;
review some basic concepts in this Python course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.280
but the Introduction to Programming course&nbsp;
will help you learn Python or any other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:37.200
programming language. Finally, get some&nbsp;
hands-on practice. Build small blocks of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:42.520
code that produce a result. In the topic after&nbsp;
next, we will show you what you need to install&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:47.800
to start learning and creating using Python.&nbsp;
Let’s move on now and get an introduction to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:53.520
Python and examine what differentiates&nbsp;
it from other programming languages.

